B, C, and an additional commit D.\n\n2. **First PR Squashed and Merged**: The first PR was merged into the main branch by squashing. This means commits A, B, and C were combined into a single new commit on the main branch. This new commit has a different commit ID than the original A, B, and C commits.\n\n3. **Merge Conflicts in Second PR**: The second PR, which still contains the original commits A, B, C, and D, now has merge conflicts. This is because the main branch has a new squashed commit that differs from the individual A, B, and C commits in the second PR.\n\nTo resolve this situation, you can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Update Your Local Main Branch**: First, make sure your local main branch is up to date with the remote main branch. This will include the new squashed commit.\n\n    ```bash\n    git checkout main\n    git pull origin main\n    ```\n\n2. **Rebase the Second PR**: Next, rebase the second PR onto the updated main branch. This will place your commit D on top of the current state of the main branch.\n\n    ```bash\n    git checkout second-pr-branch\n    git rebase main\n    ```\n\n3. **Resolve Conflicts**: During the rebase, you may encounter conflicts due to the changes in commits A, B, and C being different from the squashed commit. Resolve these conflicts manually in your code editor.\n\n4. **Continue the Rebase**: After resolving the conflicts, continue the rebase.\n\n    ```bash\n    git add .\n    git rebase --continue\n    ```\n\n5. **Force Push the Updated PR**: Once the rebase is complete and all conflicts are resolved, you will need to force push the changes to the remote branch of the second PR.\n\n    ```bash\n    git push origin second-pr-branch --force\n    ```\n\nBy doing this, your second PR will now be based on the current state of the main branch, including the squashed changes from the first PR, and only contain the additional changes from commit D. This should resolve the merge conflicts and allow for a clean merge of the sec